Hulu Battle

[Link]:
https://github.com/GoldenR1ver/JavaGameDemo

[Application scenarios]:
A 2D co-op survival game: the player controls a Hulu Brother, fights waves of demons with a melee flame cone, a charged water gun and a dodge, and picks up seeds that modify the three skills. It is also a reference implementation for anyone who wants to see how the subsystems of a game engine fit together, because there is no engine underneath. One launcher covers single player, hosting a server, joining one, hosting and joining locally for debugging, and replaying the last match.

[effectiveness]:
[1;33mAbout 7,600 lines of Java across 44 source files, plus 5 test classes and 17 JUnit tests, with no game engine underneath[0m: window, render loop, physics, input, networking and recording are all handwritten. Multiplayer is server-authoritative at 30 Hz input up and 20 Hz full snapshots down, with 30 AI actors on the field and a 40-actor cap. Each match is written as JSONL keyframes, and NetworkRoundTripTest starts a real Selector server and client to assert the full JOIN -> INPUT -> STATE -> LEFT path.

[advantage]:
The part I care about is not that it runs, but where the boundaries are drawn. Three threads never share a mutable scene: the game thread owns every Scene and GameObject, the NIO selector thread only moves bytes and session state, and the recording thread only drains a bounded queue to disk. They exchange data through concurrent queues and volatile fields, so the two classic failures -- ConcurrentModificationException from a network thread walking the scene, and a stalled selector because someone wrote to a socket inline -- cannot occur by construction. Under pressure the server degrades by dropping (512-frame outbox, 2,048-event queue, 120 inputs/s per connection) instead of blocking the game loop. The client predicts its own player, interpolates remote entities with a frame-rate-independent factor, and reconciles against the server position: snap when the error is large, ease when it is small. Protocol parsing is defensive as well -- byte-level line splitting for TCP half and sticky packets, a 16 KB line cap, name sanitization, NaN/Inf clamping, and a 30 s idle timeout. Lifecycle is equally deliberate: scene switches are deferred to the end of the frame, recording waits for the new scene to initialize, cleanup is idempotent, and every background thread is a daemon with a bounded shutdown.

[method]:
GameObject is only a component container; Component<T extends Component<T>> carries data and behavior; Scene owns add/remove queues and runs lifecycle at a safe point. The engine loop clamps delta to 50 ms so a hitch cannot tunnel objects through walls. PhysicsSystem uses up to cores - 1 daemon threads and batches work, but falls back to a serial loop below 32 bodies where scheduling costs more than the math. Rendering is LWJGL + OpenGL, with Chinese and English text drawn from an AWT-generated glyph atlas uploaded to the GPU. The wire format is a UTF-8 line protocol defined once in Protocol; snapshots are encoded on the game thread, whitelisted by entity type, and keyed by a global AtomicLong id rather than object identity, so the client can create, update and destroy remote entities deterministically. Replay uses a producer/consumer queue with 0.5 s keyframes and 10 Hz mouse sampling, and stop() drains the queue before closing the file so the tail is never truncated.

[Java]:
This is where I use the language deliberately rather than accidentally. Generics carry the ECS type contract: Component<T extends Component<T>> plus Class<T> lookup returns the right component type without an unchecked cast at every call site. Concurrency uses the right primitive for each job -- Selector, SocketChannel and ByteBuffer for non-blocking I/O; ConcurrentHashMap and ConcurrentLinkedQueue for shared session and event maps; ArrayBlockingQueue for backpressure; AtomicLong and AtomicInteger for identity; volatile for cross-thread flags; CountDownLatch for the join handshake; ExecutorService and Future for batched physics. Collections follow access patterns, such as CopyOnWriteArrayList for read-mostly components and defensive copies at the Scene boundary. Utility classes are final with private constructors, state is expressed with enums and nested types, and interrupts are restored rather than swallowed. It targets Java 11, builds with Maven, and is covered by JUnit 5 tests including a live network round trip.
